-LLR-098,SR-110,Per-module coverage-floor comparator,project-trajectory/scripts/check_coverage.py,load_floors/load_report/evaluate,"Reads the docs/coverage-floors census (module + min percent; blank and # lines ignored) and coverage.py's JSON report (per-file percent_covered, path-normalized), fails on any module below its floor or absent from the report, SKIPs when the report is absent, is a no-op with an empty census, and fails loudly on a malformed floor line.",,(see TC-101),Verified,CMP-003,1
-LLR-099,SR-054,Default-density start-collapse (T2 core),project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,know_view/sw_view/_render_drill,"The mechanized core of usability anchor T2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): a view whose scale EARNS tiering — a bundle spanning more than 3 OKF types, a module set above the component threshold (the SR-089 '>3' rule) — renders START-COLLAPSED as the shared type-tiered drill, one block per type or container, rather than a flat wall of nodes; at or below the threshold it stays flat. Covers the Knowledge, How-SW and landing What views; the What icicle start-collapses to one block per SN with descend-on-click, reusing _drill_layer_svg/_render_drill so the interaction idiom is single-sourced. SCOPE: T2's remaining 'reads legibly at default density' judgement stays perceptual under LLR-055/TC-055.","Tiering imposed on a small project costs a click to reach what already fitted on one screen, so it is earned by scale rather than switched on globally. Capping DEPTH instead of collapsing was rejected and the emitter records why: height is leaf-proportional, so stopping at the SR lane still stacks one unit per SR and the wall returns. The What icicle is the case that forced the widening — it rendered the whole spine at LEAF scale, one unit per test case.",(see TC-102),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-100,SR-054,Detail-in-context breadcrumb (T3 core),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,_render_drill/DRILL_SCRIPT,"The mechanized core of usability anchor T3 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): descending a container never replaces the view with no way back — the drill emits a declared breadcrumb nav (<nav class=""crumbs""> with a data-root-crumb root label) whose crumb click TRUNCATES the trail to that ancestor, restoring the parent view. SCOPE: the WHOLE anchor — the breadcrumb's presence AND its restore behaviour; T3 is retired as a critique anchor and no residue is left under LLR-055/TC-055.","A drill that descends with no way back strands the reader in a view they cannot leave, and 'keeps the reader oriented' never independently passed or failed a round — the breadcrumb IS that orientation, so the claim becomes testable only once it is the thing asserted. Leaving the anchor to critique was rejected because a critique CANNOT verify it: a shot matrix captures initial views only, so a round can pass on markup while the return path itself stays unverified, whereas TC-103 clicks the crumb. It follows that a critic finding T3 violated has found a gap in TC-103, which routes through change-intake to harden this test rather than through a verdict.",(see TC-103),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-101,SR-052,Non-pruning SVG container roles (A2 core),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,_svg_role/_FOCUSABLE,"The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-accessibility.md): no emitted <svg> declares the CHILDREN-PRESENTATIONAL role=img over a FOCUSABLE descendant, and any svg that does declare role=img carries its own accessible name (aria-label, or a first-direct-child <title> — per SVG-AAM a <title> nested in a <g> names the <g>, not the svg). Focusable means tabindex in any quoting OR a native SVG <a href>, which is tab-ordered without a tabindex. The role is chosen from the emitted BODY by _svg_role. SCOPE — and no wider: the container-role invariant as PROVEN by TC-104, over the artifact this repo ships plus the tiered fixture plus the predicate's own unit cases; it is NOT a proof over every reachable emitter configuration, so an emitter no rendered artifact exercises is unguarded. Name QUALITY belongs to LLR-108/TC-113.","role=img prunes its subtree from the accessibility tree, so declaring it over focusable descendants hides every one of them from assistive technology while the page still looks correct. The predicate must treat a native SVG <a href> as focusable and not tabindex alone: a tabindex-only predicate passes a diagram of linked stage cards that are tab-ordered without one. Setting the role at each call site was rejected — deriving it from the body is what stops a future emitter silently reintroducing the conflict.",(see TC-104),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-102,SR-053,One-concept-per-colour de-collision (U5 core),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS,"The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U5 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): no hex value is reused across STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS for an unrelated concept (the intentional TIER_FILL<->OKF_TYPE_FILL SN/SR/LLR/TC mirror is the one exception, asserted as a bijection rather than a set match), no PHASE_ACCENTS entry equals the CSS --accent token, and PHASE_ACCENTS clears a PAIRWISE (not merely adjacent) deltaE floor of 15. SCOPE: hex-identity and deltaE across these five declared palettes; the near-duplicate residue across vocabularies belongs to LLR-109/TC-114.","One hex carrying two concepts makes the legend a lie in both directions, and a phase sharing the --accent token makes that phase's focus and hover ring invisible — a control the reader cannot see they have reached. Comparing only ADJACENT entries was rejected: a palette is read as a whole, so two members that collide are a collision wherever they sit in the list.",(see TC-105),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-103,SR-053,Uniform node/edge/legend/highlight styling (U3 core),project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,_ring_ink/_ring_style/when_view legend,"The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U3 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): the hover/focus highlight ring resolves to ONE shared per-fill `--ring` custom property across every SVG emitter (drill/icicle/flat-DAG/knowledge) rather than a different hue per emitter, and the When tab's phase-accent key renders through the SAME `.legend`/`<i>` component every other legend in the document uses rather than a bespoke inline-chip idiom. SCOPE: highlight-token equality and legend-component equality, asserted structurally against the emitted CSS and markup. Spacing and exact visual weight belong to LLR-110/TC-115.","A highlight that changes hue between views teaches the reader a different interaction language per tab, and a legend built twice drifts in one copy while the other stays right. Per-emitter hues were the shipped state (an accent token in the drill views, a hardcoded amber elsewhere) and were rejected precisely because nothing held them equal: sharing one token means a change lands everywhere or nowhere.",(see TC-106),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-104,SR-053,Process-tab node-label type scale (U1 core),project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,--nhead/.stgt/.stgn/.hooplab/.hubname,"The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): the Process tab's working-loops SVG no longer hardcodes 12px/9.5px/13px/13px on .stgt/.stgn/.hooplab/.hubname. .stgt/.stgn are the same per-node-label role as the shared --nlabel/--nsub tokens the icicle, dag and knowledge views already share, and reuse them directly; .hooplab/.hubname are a once-per-diagram headline label, a genuinely different role, so they take the one documented --nhead scale step. SCOPE: these four selectors resolve to a declared token, asserted structurally against the emitted CSS. Whether the sizes read as uniform belongs to LLR-111/TC-116.","A magic number is invisible to every later change: two independently-drifting literals doing one job diverge the first time somebody adjusts one of them. Collapsing all four selectors onto the node-label tokens was rejected because a once-per-diagram headline is a different role, and forcing one token onto two roles is the same defect in the other direction.",(see TC-107),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-105,SR-054,Interactive-control contrast in both themes (T5 core),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,_ring_ink/_ring_style/_cedge_marker,"The mechanized core of usability anchor T5 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): EVERY control painted over a node's own fill clears the WCAG 3:1 UI-boundary floor against EVERY node fill declared in STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS, in BOTH themes — computed per fill (white or near-black, whichever contrasts more), never a single fixed hue. Covers the descend/hover/focus ring AND the containment/descend arrow (.cedge shaft plus its marker head), which derives from the same per-fill --ring token; its marker head takes a per-ink marker because marker content is rendered from the defs tree and cannot see the referencing node's custom properties (a constraint recorded in _arrow_markers), and RING_INKS is the closed ink set that makes that emission total. SCOPE: a control's contrast against its host fill, arithmetic like the sibling A4 tests. Whether the affordance otherwise reads as inviting stays perceptual under LLR-055/TC-055.","A fixed hue vanishes on whichever fill happens to match it, and the failure is total where it lands: the measured collision put a control at 1.00:1 against its host, i.e. invisible. Fixing only the ring was rejected once the same fixed token was found on the descend ARROW — the very control a reader must find to descend a tier — measuring 1.06:1 light and 1.99:1 dark on a shipped phase fill. One control's fix does not generalise; deriving every such control from the host fill does.",(see TC-108),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-106,SR-053,Single-sourced concept colour vocabulary (U2 core),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,SW_NODE_FILL/STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS,"The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md). Every concept's fill is LOOKED UP from a declared module-level palette, so the same concept cannot render two hexes — there is only one place a hex can come from. Concretely: every #rrggbb appearing in a rendered dashboard is either a member of a declared palette collection or a `--token:#hex` theme definition (surfaces, text and borders, which encode no concept). SCOPE: this proves single-sourcing of the VOCABULARY, not that a concept was assigned a well-chosen hue — collision and perceptual separation belong to LLR-102.","A vocabulary is one vocabulary only if every member is in it: a bare hex literal outside the declared collection is invisible to the collision sweep and to the ring-ink enumeration alike, so it evades both the checks that would catch it. That is not hypothetical — a four-kind vocabulary declared only three because one badge fill sat inline in its emitter. Auditing hues by review was rejected: review cannot see a member that was never declared.",(see TC-109),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-107,SR-053,One interaction idiom per node role (U4 core),project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,_drill_layer_svg/arch_icicle/dag_svg/know_graph_svg node groups,"The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U4 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md). Two node roles exist and each is spelled ONE way in every emitter that renders it: an IDENTIFIED node (icicle .cell, flat-DAG .wi, knowledge .knode) advertises its registry id through `data-id`; a DETAIL-BEARING drill node (.block, in both the When and How-SW drills) advertises its detail-map key through `data-node`; and a node of either role is made focusable the same way, `tabindex=""0""` on the group. Neither role ever carries the other's attribute. SCOPE: ATTRIBUTE parity, not runtime behaviour parity.","One node addressed two ways means every later consumer must handle both, and the second spelling is the one that gets forgotten. Proving that two emitters' handlers BEHAVE alike was considered and rejected as out of reach without a browser harness — what is provable from the markup is that they are wired through one vocabulary, and that is the narrowing this row accepts explicitly rather than claiming more than it checks.",(see TC-110),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-108,SR-052,"Accessible-name quality, not merely presence (A2 residue)",project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,_render_drill breadcrumb label; every focusable/role=img emit site,"The QUALITY half of accessibility anchor A2, mechanizing what LLR-101 leaves perceptual. Three rules over every focusable element and role=img graphic: it HAS an accessible name; that name is never a bare registry id; and navigation LANDMARKS are distinct. SCOPE, narrower than a first measurement suggests: A2 governs INTERACTIVE elements and meaningful graphics — a <title> on a decorative edge path is a tooltip, not an accessible name. Uniqueness is asserted for landmarks ONLY: a descend control for the same container legitimately recurs across drill layers, one visible at a time, so those repeats are one control reached by different paths. Whether a well-formed, unique, descriptive name is the RIGHT description belongs to SR-054's reading-task critique.","A bare registry id tells a screen-reader user nothing about what the control is, and a reader listing landmarks hears a flat list in which two called 'Breadcrumb' are indistinguishable — which is what three drills produce unless the names are made distinct. Asserting over decorative graphics too was rejected: it would manufacture findings the accessibility standard does not make, and a check that cries wolf is one a reader stops reading.",(see TC-113),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-109,SR-053,Pairwise deltaE floor across every vocabulary (U5 residue),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS,"Mechanizes what LLR-102 leaves perceptual: a PAIRWISE deltaE floor across every declared vocabulary, not only within PHASE_ACCENTS. Two floors, each a recorded judgement — 15 WITHIN a vocabulary (every member can sit beside every other in one legend) and 12 ACROSS (two vocabularies share no legend, so the floor is lower rather than absent). The declared tier<->okf mirror is exempt BY DESIGN and is asserted BYTE-EQUAL, so 'exempt' cannot drift into 'nearly equal'. SCOPE: perceptual SEPARATION of the declared palettes. Whether a given hue suits its concept is not asserted.","'A collision the identity check misses' looked like a judgement call only because the formula was already there and nobody had widened the set it ran over — the same arithmetic on more members. A cross-vocabulary floor of zero was rejected on evidence: a reader conflated a pair drawn from two different vocabularies, so sharing no legend reduces the risk without removing it. Three live conflations were sitting in the shipped palettes at the binding — sw external/component at deltaE 8.6, SR/phase[3] at 9.5, Interface/phase[7] at 10.5.",(see TC-114),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-110,SR-053,Declared weight/alpha/corner tokens (U3 residue),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,--w-*/--o-*/--r-* tokens; SVG_RX,"Mechanizes what LLR-103 leaves perceptual. Fourteen CSS tokens plus SVG_RX name one step per role for stroke-width, opacity and corner radius, near-duplicates merged into the role's step, and every emitted value must resolve to a declared token. stroke-opacity is checked under the opacity scale deliberately — it is the same scale applied to a stroke. SVG_RX is enforced against both the source literals and every render, because the rx presentation attribute cannot read a CSS custom property portably. SCOPE: token membership, asserted over paint surfaces (style blocks and style/fill/stroke attributes), never over prose that quotes CSS.","'Spacing and exact visual weight' was undecidable because the weights were never declared: the shipped page carried 8 stroke-widths, 7 opacities, 5 corner radii and 6 SVG rx values, of which five stroke widths were doing the single job 'draw a connector' and four rx values 'round a node box'. Nothing can be uniform against an undeclared scale, so declaring the roles is what turns the judgement into a check; leaving it to review was rejected because a near-duplicate weight is exactly what an eye does not catch.",(see TC-115),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-111,SR-053,"Declared type scale, no raw font-size literals (U1 residue)",project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,--nlabel/--nsub/--nhead + --tiny/--xsmall/--small/--body/--lead/--display/--hero + --rel,"Mechanizes what LLR-104 left perceptual. 'Do the sizes read as visually uniform' was undecidable because the scale was never written down: 18 raw font-size literals against 5 tokens, with .7/.75rem, .9/.95/.98rem, 1.05/1.1rem and 8.5/9px each being near-duplicate steps for ONE role 3-7% apart. Eleven steps are now declared in THREE documented families - node (px, because SVG geometry is fixed px and a rem resizes labels out of their boxes), page (rem), and one relative step (em, for text sized against its parent) - and every emitted font-size must resolve to one. Claiming a single scale across those units would be false, so the LLR states three. SCOPE: step membership over paint surfaces. Whether the chosen steps are well-proportioned is not asserted.",,(see TC-116),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-112,SR-052,Keyboard reachability of every wired control (A1 core),project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,emitted querySelectorAll wiring; tabindex + native-link emission,"The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-accessibility.md): every element the emitted page WIRES an interaction to is keyboard-focusable. The wired set is DERIVED from the document's own DOM-selecting calls and then classified — a control selector (its matches receive per-element click/focus/dblclick/keydown listeners) may match only elements carrying tabindex=0 or natively tab-ordered (a button; an SVG a-with-href, the LLR-101 predicate), and each non-control selector records why its matches are not focus stops. A newly wired selector fails the owning test until classified. The derivation reads EVERY DOM-selecting API (querySelector/querySelectorAll/closest/matches, any quote style) and fails loudly on a non-literal selector argument, which a static read cannot classify. A control-selector wiring loop that attaches click or dblclick must attach focus or keydown beside it, statically asserted. Order-integrity: document order IS emission order, no positive tabindex may reorder the walk, and tabindex=-1 is legal only on the roving tablist buttons. SCOPE — and no wider: focusability, order-integrity and activation PAIRING of the wired set over the shipped artifact plus the seven-fixture emitter sweep. Runtime behaviour equivalence is not driven, and whether the order FEELS sensible stays a reader-experience judgement. Runtime-created controls (the breadcrumb buttons) are native <button>s, keyboard-operable by construction and outside the static walk — a recorded limit.","A control reachable only by pointer is unreachable for a keyboard user, and the page still looks complete, so nothing reports it. Enumerating the controls by hand was rejected: a per-element list goes stale the moment a selector is added, whereas deriving the wired set from the document's own calls means a new selector fails until someone classifies it. Two narrowings are load-bearing and must not be relaxed back: the derivation has to read every DOM-selecting API rather than one spelling of one call, or it silently misses wired controls; and the key-handler half has to be asserted statically rather than deferred to a browser, or mouse-only wiring ships.",(see TC-117),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-113,SR-052,No information by colour alone (A3 core),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,tier_legend + sw/phase/status legend emitters; STATUS_GLYPH,"The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A3: for every declared colour vocabulary (enumerated from the module constants, the U5/U2 closure, so a new vocabulary enters by existing), every member that actually PAINTS in a rendered document resolves to a legend swatch labelled in words in that same document, with CSS tokens resolved. Status members additionally pair a per-status glyph. The emitted JS detail-badge maps are SUBSTITUTED from TIER_FILL/STATUS_FILL rather than hand-copied, and three drift guards hold every copy equal to the constants: the emitted JS maps byte-match the Python palettes; the CSS status tokens match STATUS_FILL and are asserted theme-invariant; and the tier-legend bijection derives its expectation from the TIER_FILL dict, so a member dropped from the legend also fails. SCOPE and its narrowing: the worded cue must exist in the same document — that it sits within eyeshot of the painted element is layout this does not re-assert. The JS detail-panel badge stays out of the LEGEND rule because its background carries its concept as the badge's own visible text.","Colour alone excludes any reader who cannot resolve it, and the failure is silent: the page looks informative to everyone who can. A raw hex scan was rejected as the detection method because tokenised fills under-report it — the status legend paints through a CSS variable, and a hex scan finds one member of four. The substitution and the three guards exist because a hand-copied palette copy is the recurring defect here: one stale hex shipped in three separate places, and each copy that a test shares with its emitter is a copy the test cannot catch. The same-document narrowing costs a reader nothing today (every painted member is cued in its own tab panel) and a re-attestation should bless it knowingly.",(see TC-118),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-114,SR-052,"Text-contrast floors, closed over the vocabulary set (A4)",project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS + _ring_ink,"Accessibility anchor A4 bound. The WCAG 2.1 AA arithmetic is owned by six sibling tests — node fills vs label ink at 4.5:1, the emitted badge/boundary pairs, the no-opacity-discount rule, theme-token fills behind white text in BOTH themes, the hub-is-not-accent regression, and the per-fill focus ring at the 3:1 boundary floor — so this row adds the BINDING plus the gap none of them owned: the SET. Closed by reflection: every UPPERCASE module-level collection holding hex members must be one of the five declared vocabularies, and every member of the REFLECTED set clears 4.5:1 against its label ink (white, except the one declared queued dark-ink exception) and 3:1 for its computed focus ring. The walk is RECURSIVE (nested dicts, tuples, sets, bare strings), counts any 3/4/6/8-digit hex form as hex-bearing, and requires every member be CANONICAL 6-digit lowercase. The theme-token half is DERIVED from the emitted CSS: every fill:var(--x) token must carry a declared role, white-text-fill roles clear 4.5:1 under white in BOTH themes, container-fill roles keep --text readable in both. SCOPE — and no wider: contrast arithmetic over declared constants and emitted theme tokens (the ring boundary also binds under SR-054 T5 as LLR-105); a lowercase module-level palette would evade the reflection and is forbidden by the module's UPPERCASE-constant convention, a recorded reliance. Rendered-pixel measurement is not asserted.","Each sibling test sweeps a hand-copied list of the palettes, so a sixth vocabulary could ship outside every floor check while all six stayed green — the arithmetic was right and the SET was the hole. A new vocabulary must enter the arithmetic by EXISTING rather than by being remembered, which is what reflection buys and a seventh hand-copied list would not. Two shapes of that same defect must not return: a flat-dict-only walk misses nested and non-dict collections, and a hand-listed theme-token set is the very defect this row claims to close — both were live here, which is why the walk is recursive and the token set derived.",(see TC-119),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-115,SR-054,Task findability - labelled entry within one tab switch (T1 core),project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,build_html/_next_work_html/_nav,"The mechanized core of usability anchor T1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). Each of the three core reading tasks the rubric concretizes — find the project state, find the next work, find how the parts connect — is reachable within one tab switch of the landing view, its entry point a LABELLED nav control or a NAMED surface on the landing view, never an unlabelled region a reader must discover by clicking. THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT that makes the check honest: it is judged against the RENDERED artifact carrying real registry data, NEVER the nav skeleton. The guard resolves each task's entry point from the emitted document (task 1 -> the landing Definition-completeness/Execution cards, 0 switches; task 2 -> the landing Next-work card, 0 switches; task 3 -> the How-SW nav tab, 1 switch), asserts each is LABELLED and within one switch, and asserts the next-work surface is NON-EMPTY on the zero-active/all-queued fixture. SCOPE — PROVEN: a labelled entry point per task, reachable in one switch, and the next-work surface non-empty across the practice fixtures (zero-active, waiting-with-predecessor, drained). DELIBERATELY NOT: whether the reader subjectively knows what to do next; T1 leaves the critique entirely, with no residue under LLR-055/TC-055.","'Obvious' was always glossed as labelled-rather-than-unlabelled, and that gloss is the whole bar — once it is written operationally there is nothing perceptual left to adjudicate, so the anchor can leave the critique instead of being re-judged every round. The rendered-artifact constraint is the load-bearing part and must not be relaxed to a structural check: a next-work surface can EXIST in the emitter and render nothing when no row is active, so a nav-skeleton check passes on exactly the artifact a reader finds empty.",(see TC-120),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-116,SR-054,Viewport-fit by scale-to-fit with a legibility floor (T7 core),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py,_svg_fit_style/SHRINK_FLOOR,"The mechanized core of usability anchor T7 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). Every emitted SVG (all three wrappers: _svg_wrap for dag/sw/know, the icicle panel, _drill_layer_svg) carries width:100% + max-width:<natural> + min-width:<natural x SHRINK_FLOOR> instead of a FIXED pixel width, so a diagram fills its container and scales down as the viewport narrows. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowing: a view whose natural width exceeds 390 / SHRINK_FLOOR still scrolls horizontally WITH its cue — the row's own fallback rule made mechanical. Closing that remainder needs a narrow-width re-layout (stacked columns), which is not this row.","A fixed pixel width pins the rendered size whatever the container does, which is why every narrow view demanded sideways scroll and one graph clipped a component label mid-word. A viewBox alone cannot fix it — the fixed width still wins. The legibility floor exists because pure scale-to-fit TRADES this anchor for T4: a 900px graph squeezed into 390px shrinks a label past readable, so the floor stops the fix from creating the defect it is next to.",(see TC-121),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-117,SR-054,Theme-lock: one theme mechanism and no mixed-family surface/ink pair (T6 core),project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,HTML_TEMPLATE theme tokens + every emitter's paint,"The mechanized core of usability anchor T6 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). The measurable content is FAMILY PAIRING, not the absence of literals: a fixed status swatch carrying fixed white ink is theme-locked CORRECTLY, since neither side flips. A page inverts mid-view in exactly two ways and this row forbids both. (1) A SECOND THEME MECHANISM below :root — the document declares exactly one prefers-color-scheme block, targeting :root, and one color-scheme, also at :root. (2) A MIXED PAIR — a theme-varying ink on a theme-invariant surface, or the reverse. The two families are DERIVED from the emitted document (the dark block's override set IS the definition of theme-varying), never hand-listed, and the dark block may not override a token :root never declares. Applied to CSS rules declaring both a background and an ink, to every emitted SVG node pairing an inline rect fill with an inline text fill, and to CSS-declared SVG text fills paired against the family their host nodes actually paint in. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowings: --ring is excluded because it is stamped FROM the host fill (LLR-105 owns its floor); the JS-composed badge idiom pairs background and ink at runtime from the invariant vocabulary, where LLR-114's arithmetic owns it; and the page CSS has exactly ONE rule declaring a classifiable background AND ink today (body), so that half is prospective while the sweep's weight sits on the node and text-fill halves. Whether the chosen palettes are PLEASANT stays perceptual.","A per-component theme override is a seam the reader crosses while scrolling, and a mixed pair reads correctly in the theme it was authored in and inverts in the other — both are invisible to the author working in one theme. Forbidding colour LITERALS was the obvious rule and is the wrong one: it fails a correct fixed-on-fixed pairing and passes a varying-on-invariant one, so it would flag the safe case and miss the defect. Deriving the families from the emitted document rather than listing them means a new token joins a family by existing.",(see TC-122),Verified,CMP-002,3
-LLR-118,SR-049,Generated owner decision surface (registry + attestation depth),project-trajectory/scripts/gen_open_items.py,render/_attestation_cards/word_diff/mask_local,"The RENDERED half of the Modified/Draft attestation regime whose gate SR-049 derives. gen_open_items renders (a) every pending row of docs/requirements/open-items.csv as a decision brief and (b) every SR whose Status is Draft (ratification owed) or Modified (re-attest owed) with its whole chain's per-cell before/after. Two contracts the emitted file carries: the attestation-baseline STAMP (a --since render must be reproducible by the freshness gate, or --check compares against a different history) and the check-the-baseline empty state (an auto-derived baseline sitting AFTER a pre-regime amendment renders no cells, which must never read as nothing-changed). The machine-local advisory region is masked before comparing. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowings: this module RENDERS and owns no second opinion — the attestation model is trace.reattest_model and the pending pointers are gen_trajectory.pending_block, both imported, so a disagreement with `trace.py --ratify` means this view is the defect and the brief wins. NOT claimed: that the rendered page is legible or well-composed.","A human cannot bless a delta they cannot see, and the depth that makes a re-attest readable is a WORD-LEVEL diff — which the markdown surface this replaces could not mark, so a reviewer was asked to sign a change they could only read as a whole cell. Re-deriving the attestation model here was rejected: a second opinion that disagreed with the brief would leave the reviewer arbitrating between two surfaces. Both emitted contracts exist because their absence fails SILENTLY in front of someone about to attest, which is the worst place for a quiet failure.",(see TC-123),Verified,CMP-002,4
-LLR-119,SR-054,"T4 label legibility, mechanized: ink inside its box, truncation with a reveal",project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,_drill_block_label/_fit_lines/_next_work_title,"The mechanized floor of usability anchor T4 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md), widening the anchor's existing owner rather than adding a rubric anchor. The clause made objective: no character a block renders may fall outside that block's own rect, horizontally or vertically. The emitter FITS the sub-label to the column it will actually be drawn in — a second line when one will not hold it, then an ellipsis — with three text lines the ceiling, reusing the grid the id/name wrap idiom already proved fits row_h; a sub-label that already fits one line renders byte-identically to before. SCOPE — PROVEN: for every block of every drill layer in the emitted document, each label line's ink box lies inside its own node rect, measured from the emitted geometry with the emitter's declared per-character widths and the document's declared font sizes, entities counted as the single glyph they render. Second half: this emitter does not truncate the next-work title by a fixed budget — the card emits the whole clause and lets its own width do the fitting, the declared bound _NEXT_WORK_TITLE caps ONE item's HEIGHT rather than its text, and where it bites the remainder discloses through a NATIVE <details> cut at a word so head and remainder rejoin continuously when opened. DELIBERATELY NOT: whether an ellipsis a reader meets is ACTIONABLE — truncated-without-affordance stays T4's perceptual residue under LLR-055/TC-055, so T4 remains a live critique anchor; the full string keeps its homes in the block's title, aria-label and data-summary.","A label is illegible AS a label the moment its ink leaves the box it names, which is the half of this anchor a reader cannot argue with. Column right-sizing (LLR-057, SR-056) cannot absorb it: right-sizing clamps at MAX_TIER_COL, so a 400-character need asks for a 2000px column and gets 172 — the overflow is structural, not a tuning problem. A FIXED character budget was the shipped approach on the next-work card and is rejected: it spends the same budget at every width, cutting a title mid-word on a wide screen with the card half empty. Native <details> is the point of the reveal — operable by pointer AND keyboard with no script, in a file opened from disk, which is what makes the affordance assertable from the markup instead of needing a browser. The height bound is measured over 320 registry rows: median 44, p90 126, max 609 characters of leading clause.",(see TC-124),Verified,CMP-002,4
-LLR-120,SR-054,No edge through an unrelated node box (T8 objective floor),project-trajectory/scripts/traj_graph.py,_route_edges/_detour_d/_polyline_hits,"The mechanized floor of usability anchor T8 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). T8 is two clauses and this row claims only the first: NO EDGE PASSES THROUGH AN UNRELATED NODE BOX. SCOPE — PROVEN: across the shipped dashboard plus one fixture per emitter, every emitted wire, sampled as the polyline a reader's eye follows, clears every node rect that is not its own source or target. MEASURED at the binding: 264 shipped wires, 0 through a box. DELIBERATELY NOT: crossing count, lane separation, or where a crossing falls — that residue stays under LLR-055/TC-055, which is why T8 remains a live critique anchor. Separately and NOT claimed here: at the router's own 3px clearance rather than the bare box, one wire does graze, so _detour_d exhausting its lane candidates and returning the least-bad fallback is a reachable state rather than a theoretical one; that wire's identity and the lane-separation work belong to the open work item.","A wire crossing an unrelated box reads as a connection that is not there, which is a wrong answer rather than an untidy one — geometry a reader cannot argue with. The claim about the ARTIFACT is deliberately separate from the router's own unit tests and is why this row exists: _detour_d returns a LEAST-BAD fallback when no candidate lane clears, so 'the algorithm detours' does not imply 'nothing shipped through a box', and the artifact-level claim had only ever been hand-verified once.",(see TC-125),Verified,CMP-002,4
-LLR-121,SR-111,Kit-version stamp,project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py,write_kit_version,"write_kit_version stamps docs/kit-version (SHA + date, -dirty on an uncommitted kit tree) so a re-sync is diffable.",Split from the idempotent-write row: the two obligations share a module but not a failure mode — a scaffold can write correctly and still leave the adopter unable to say what they scaffolded from.,(see TC),Verified,CMP-005,1
-LLR-122,SR-113,dev-setup wires core.hooksPath,project-trajectory/scripts/dev-setup.template.sh,dev-setup,"dev-setup wires the pre-commit floor (core.hooksPath) from its baseline, so the floor is active from the setup the contributor actually runs.","Split from the template row: reaching a green setup and being protected by the process floor fail independently, and the second is the one a contributor never notices is missing.",(see TC),Verified,CMP-005,1
-LLR-123,SR-115,Deterministic traincar ordering,project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py,frontier/evaluate,"Orders eligible traincars by (gate, Priority desc, downstream-dependent count desc, hard-path desc, first WI id).","Split from the frontier row: deriving WHICH traincars are eligible and deciding WHICH runs first fail independently, and only the second one makes a run replayable.",(see TC),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-124,SR-116,Generated status + run-state,project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py,REGEN_STEPS/regen,"docs/status.md's generated block is maintained by the trunk lane only: trunk_step --regen runs gen_trajectory --status after derive_gate (the gate is its input), and the status-map freshness gate byte-compares it on the trunk while work branches skip (SS5.2). (docs/run-state and its four-valued dispatcher outcome retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5.)","Split from the retirement row: removing the old pointer and generating the surfaces that replace it are separable, and the second is what keeps the replacement from rotting the same way.",(see TC),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-130,SR-122,Dashboard staleness check,project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py,main/_asof/ASOF_RE,Implements stale/missing --check with a git as-of stamp excluded from stamp-only comparison.,"Split from the determinism row: determinism is what makes the check possible, but a deterministic artifact nobody re-checks still goes stale.",(see TC),Verified,CMP-002,1
-LLR-131,SR-124,Contradiction-safe dual-plan class,project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py,classify,Derives contradiction-safe single-WI scheduling from PlanMode and quarantines a contradictory SafetyClass.,"Split from the path-isolation row: refusing a wrong lane and deriving the right one are separate, and only the second sees a registry contradiction.",(see TC),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-132,SR-125,PAGE ratification-level mapping,project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py,main,"Applies the declared level's continuation semantics to a PAGE at both the dispatcher disposition and the --dual-plan single-shot round: `page_action(human_held, keep_nondependent)` over the four cells the retired enum's three words could not span — a human-held tier pages, a loop-held one STALLs, and `None` normalizes to the strictest cell rather than to `bool(None)`'s most permissive one.","Split from the disposition row: committing the outcome and deciding whether it stops a human are independent, and the second is shared with a second entry point that would otherwise drift.",(see TC),Modified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-133,SR-126,Spine stand-alone rule,project-trajectory/scripts/trace_text.py,provenance_findings,"provenance_findings flags a WI id or a process-doc citation across the normative cells of all three spine registries, joining the --strict failure set via exit_code.","A citation is a pointer a later reader may be unable to follow, and the row's reason travels with it: when the work item is closed and archived, the row keeps its claim and loses its justification, which is the failure this catches. Applied to every spine registry rather than the SR alone, because the layer the rule could not originally see held the largest pocket of violations — a narrower scope would guard the cleanest layer and miss the dirtiest.",(see TC),Verified,CMP-001,1
-LLR-134,SR-127,Requirement form rules,project-trajectory/scripts/trace_text.py,form_findings,"form_findings applies six rules — one obligation per requirement, no non-obligation modal, no actorless passive, no unfalsifiable term, no open-ended clause, no obligation keyword in an LLR Detail — skipping Draft rows, and joins the --strict failure set via exit_code.","A row carrying two obligations reports neither when one fails, and undecidable wording is signed on impression rather than evidence — that is the failure all six rules exist to prevent. Draft is skipped because a Draft row is exempt from the child-completeness rules elsewhere in this harness and is by convention the state a requirement occupies while still being written, so an unfinished acceptance criterion is expected there rather than defective; flagging it would make the state unusable for the drafting it exists to allow. This cell says ""obligation keyword"" rather than quoting the keyword itself because the rule cannot distinguish a mention from a use, and an exemption a checker cannot see is one an author can reach for.",(see TC),Verified,CMP-001,1
-LLR-135,SR-128,Paraphrase advisory,project-trajectory/scripts/trace_text.py,paraphrase_advisories,"paraphrase_advisories compares a child cell's significant words (alphabetic runs of four characters or more, lowercased) against its parent's and reports when the share of the CHILD's words that also appear in the parent exceeds the declared threshold — the child is the denominator, so a long parent cannot dilute a short echoing child. Reported in its own advisory pipe, which never joins a failure set.","A child that re-words its parent adds a second home for one fact and no decomposition, so a later amendment updates one copy and the two disagree with nothing to report it. The advisory has its own pipe rather than sharing the acceptance-criteria counter because a shared count would report 'ac-advisories' about a finding that is not one, and a reader acts on the counter name.",(see TC),Verified,CMP-001,1
-LLR-136,SR-129,Registry converter (CSV <-> spec folder),project-trajectory/scripts/wi_convert.py,to_specs/to_csv/verify/write_spec_file,"Converts between the registry homes per the Phase 2a grammar (TOML +++ frontmatter, Deliverable in the body, status from the directory, order key only as a CSV-order migration artifact). --verify round-trips CSV->specs->CSV in a temp dir, exits nonzero on any cell mismatch, and reports byte identity with a reason. write_spec_file is the single spec writer (to_specs and plan_artifacts filing both go through it). Refuses unknown statuses by row, non-empty targets without --force, and any in-flight active/<branch>/ claim by name (status is the FIRST path component, the loaders' rule; conversion is a drained-stop operation per §3.2 - the Phase 4 claim protocol made the state reachable).",One writer keeps migration and filing byte-compatible; refusal over catch-all keeps a malformed registry loud rather than silently reclassified.,(see TC-129),Verified,CMP-005,4
-LLR-137,SR-130,Serial trunk step (log compile + regen),project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py,compile_log/ordered_fragments/rebase_links,"Compiles docs/log.d/*.md into docs/log.md via plan_artifacts.append_log_summary in (git add-time, filename) order - all fragments validated first (name shape, ## heading, reserved section headings refused, an adding commit required - on HEAD, or on MERGE_HEAD during the integrator's merge composition), relative links rebased from docs/log.d/ to docs/, fragments deleted after append, all-or-nothing on any validation error. Never commits; the caller owns the commit. The artifact-regen half is LLR-142 (SR-134).",One serial actor per §5.5.,(see TC-130),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-138,SR-131,Tracked pause reader (dual-home),project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py,tracked_pause/pause_reason,"pause_reason reads the ONE tracked home docs/work/pause via tracked_pause (TOML reason/since; malformation fails closed to the pinned loud placeholder). The legacy untracked lane/pause half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5 - a stray local file is inert, pinned by test.","A broken pause file must still pause; two homes during the migration window, one meaning.",(see TC-131),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-139,SR-135,Pause surfaced in the pending block,project-trajectory/scripts/traj_status.py,_pause_pending/pending_block,"Renders zero-or-one 'Paused since <since> - <reason>' bullet in the committed-tree-pure pending region from docs/work/pause - declared since verbatim, no clock, the since clause omitted when empty, fail-closed text on malformation pinned equal to the reader's placeholder.","An open pause is a visible accruing cost, never a forgotten one.",(see TC-136),Verified,CMP-002,4
-LLR-140,SR-132,Local integrator (claim + queue + audit),project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py,claim/finished_branches/_verdict_gate/integrate_one/audit,"claim: §2.3 steps 1+2 with the refusal ladder (tracked pause via agent_common.tracked_pause, dirty tree, existing branch, unsafe name, non-ordinary safety_class, off-frontier id) before a git mv + trunk commit + branch cut. finished_branches: a claimed branch whose tip holds no spec under active/<branch>/ (the closing move IS the signal). integrate_one: --no-ff --no-commit merge, trunk_step.py folded into the merge commit so it passes its own floor, check.py --jobs 0 at the declared tier on the composed tree read fail-closed (nonzero exit OR any SKIP line refuses), verdict gate per docs/review-policy using score_reviews.parse_verdict with the verdict's last branch commit required no older than the last non-review non-fragment commit, ff-only trunk advance, branch deleted on green, candidate parked on red. audit: git log --first-parent --no-merges over --since..HEAD; paths outside docs/work/, docs/log.d/, docs/log.md and the stack.ini [generated] set flag the commit.","One serial actor, one page; every refusal names its reason; the queue stops at the first red rather than composing past it.",(see TC-132),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-141,SR-133,Work-branch lane signal + freshness skip,project-trajectory/scripts/check.py,_claimed_work_branch/steps,"On a claimed work branch the _TRUNK_FRESHNESS_STEPS set SKIPs with a §5.2 notice, exit code untouched; the plan listing still names every step. The claim signal answers in two stages - docs/work/active/<branch>/ on disk (the fast path), else the branch's own committed history (git log -1 -- docs/work/active/<branch>) - so the closing commit that archives the claim, and every commit after it, keep the skip. No git, a failing git, or an empty answer all read as the trunk lane: a false work-branch positive would switch the freshness gates off on the one branch that owns regenerating them.","A false work-branch positive would relax the trunk's freshness bar, so every ambiguity resolves toward the trunk lane.",(see TC-134),Verified,CMP-001,4
-LLR-142,SR-134,Trunk-step artifact regen (dependency order),project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py,regen,"Regenerates arch-map, okf, derived-gate, dashboard, status block and open-items as subprocesses in dependency order (derived-gate before its two consumers), skipping absent artifact families with a notice and stopping loudly at the first failure. Never commits; the caller owns the commit.","derive_gate runs before the dashboard/status regens because docs/gate is their input, not their output.",(see TC-135),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-143,SR-026,Plain-launch drive loop (claim->build->integrate),project-trajectory/scripts/drive.py,run/_resume_or_claim/_stranded_claims/_default_worker,"The serial scheduling front end a plain agent_loop.py launch runs: each cycle re-derives the ready frontier (schedule.frontier), refuses on the tracked pause and on a dirty trunk at the cycle top, resumes a parked claimed branch else claims the next queued WI in build order (integrate.claim, branch name check-ref-format-validated), launches one worker session on the claimed branch's worktree (agent_loop.py --worktree --wi with the session dials forwarded), drains the serial merge queue (integrate.integrate) counting merges from finished_branches, and stops loudly on any composed refusal - stranded claims (active specs with no branch ref) refuse by name, NEEDS-HUMAN propagates as exit 7, a stall aborts, an empty frontier drains to exit 0. The session-config preflight applies only when work needs a worker. Never pushes.","Ordering only, no new authority: every refusal stays where it already lives, and the driver is ~400 lines of composition against the 4,042-line dispatcher it replaces.",(see TC-137),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-144,SR-132,Terminal lane outcomes (handback + quarantine),project-trajectory/scripts/handback.py,close_partial/quarantine/report_path/render_report/_restore/diff_records,"close_partial: commits the lane's work as-is, moves each claimed spec to the TERMINAL partial/ (nothing re-claims it, so nothing strands) and writes ONE IMMUTABLE per-close report under docs/handbacks/ naming the claimed outcome, the reason, the commit range and the keep/discard split. The report is the close EVENT's identity — immutable so it never moves, unique so a second close is a second document — which is what dissolved the starvation class five mutable-proxy dedup mechanisms died on. A second close is refused rather than overwriting the first, and any refusal restores every report the call wrote (schedule reads queued+blockref as blocked) and the driver cannot claim and return it forever. quarantine: the ruled red arm - reverts the product paths to the merge base and keeps the failing diff as a bar-inert .patch under docs/work/handback/, reading `git diff --name-status -z` as three-field RECORDS so a rename cannot revert the wrong path, checking every git return code, and restoring rather than reporting when a revert step fails. Both leave the branch finished, so integrate.branch_outcomes reads the outcome off the spec's DIRECTORY - complete/=merged, cancelled/=cancelled, any open folder=handback - and every lane ends in a merge.",Making the outcome the directory leaves no fourth answer and no state file that could hold one; partial work lands in trunk where a future WI can find it instead of on a branch nobody will look at.,(see TC-138),Modified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-145,SR-132,Link-aware spec-move ritual (move + relink indivisible),project-trajectory/scripts/spec_move.py,move_spec/expected_relink/archive_dest,"move_spec moves a registry/spec file and keeps every markdown link in the repo true as ONE indivisible operation: the moved file's own relative targets are re-relativised against its new directory, every inbound link whose target resolves to the vacated path is redirected (link text untouched, #fragments carried, external/protocol-relative/bare-anchor targets left alone, root-relative targets excused only in the rebase half, line endings preserved via newline=''), and every rewritten file is staged with the move so the ritual lands as one changeset. integrate.claim and handback.close_partial call it for the claim and close moves; the CLI performs the worker's terminal close move and the dated docs/archive/specs/ archival. expected_relink is the pure oracle integrate._abandoned_claim compares byte-for-byte to excuse a crashed claim's own relink writes without widening its conviction to any .md edit.","A bare move strands both link directions and the break surfaces late, on the composed tree (measured: one probe archival took the broken-link count from 4 to 8 - 3 outbound, 1 inbound). Making move+relink one indivisible operation is a constraint that leaves the two-thirds state unrepresentable, rather than a check that detects it after the parallel work is done.",(see TC-139),Modified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-146,SR-136,Declared-figure provenance presence check,project-trajectory/scripts/check_figures.py,findings_for/judge_marker/marker_segments,"Scans root *.md + docs/**/*.md (check_doc_refs.doc_files - the same walk, so the two doc checks agree on the surface and generated trees stay skipped) plus docs/stack.ini, with docs/reviews/ records out of scope (a verdict quotes defective markers as evidence); each opt-in fig: marker is judged on its own attributes and must carry a non-empty, wordful cmd= and rev= pair (rev bare or quoted), or a non-empty derived= derivation; a marker is grammar prose that declares nothing only when every attribute value is wholly a placeholder token (angle-bracket-wrapped, or the bare ellipsis - so the convention text ships scaffold-clean); in a judged marker a placeholder-shaped value counts as absent, never as satisfying (an unfilled rev=<revision> names no revision), while a metacharacter inside a longer value is command text and the marker is judged on its attributes; findings name the missing half and quote the line; GENERATED marker blocks and fig-ok lines are exempt; warn-first, --strict exits 1. Presence only, never truth - re-derivation is rung 2, deliberately not built.","Would have flagged all three 2026-08-01 false figures; opt-in keeps the claim honest - declared figures carry provenance, not all figures do.",(see TC-140),Verified,CMP-003,1
-LLR-147,SR-049,SN-coverage gate rung + uncovered basis count,project-trajectory/scripts/derive_gate.py,sn_gate/sn_cited_ids,"sn_gate reads the cited set sn_cited_ids builds from SR SN-Refs (an F5 duplicate of trace.py's parse, pinned equal by test_rule_sync like phase_num and LLR_EXEMPT): a RATIFIED SN cited by zero SRs caps the raw level at G0 - a ratified-but-unanswered need has not earned G1 - while a Draft SN stays exempt from the coverage rung and rides the existing draft rung (one fact, one finding; the itemized 'SN has no SR' listing remains trace.py's orphan rule at G2 strictness, reading the SAME cited set, so the gate input and the listing cannot contradict on one registry state). _raw_level rebuilds the cited set from the rows in scope, so the ex-draft counterfactual drops a Draft SR's citation with its row and never fabricates coverage. The compared basis line gains uncovered=N (ratified-and-uncited count) beside drafts=N/modified=N - additive for readers, but --check compares the line whole, so it is a cache-format change downstream repos regenerate through.","Before this rung SN->SR linkage was not a gate input at all: an unanswered ratified need left the gate untouched, orphaning only at G2 strictness (owner ruling 2026-08-01, dispatcher-flow review Q2). Measured safe before landing: the census on this repo read 25/25 SNs covered and zero ghost SN-Refs, so the rung lands green here and the uncovered case is constructed by fixture, not found.",(see TC-141),Verified,CMP-001,4
-LLR-148,SR-049,Derived next phase as an output mode,project-trajectory/scripts/derive_gate.py,main,"--next-phase prints max(Phase over non-draft spine rows) + 1 - the basis line's phase=N derivation exposed as an output mode (no second parse, docs/gate never written), bare so the intake mint helper and any agent can int() the answer when a confirmed scope change opens a new phase; a Draft row's phase is not yet scope and never bumps the answer; an unphased spine is the implicit foundation (1), so it prints 2. The boundary it serves is ruled (owner 2026-08-01): a phase increments on an adjudication-confirmed scope change or a ratified draft-SN batch, never on the raw derived-gate drop.","One derived call keeps the boundary number mechanical - hand-picked numbers drift, and a spurious Modified window must not burn a phase number (the counterexample: 19 traced cells once flipped 11 SRs with no scope moved).",(see TC-142),Verified,CMP-001,4
-LLR-149,SR-093,§A8 admission: the kind x ratification-level table + the spine barrier,project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py,_kind_action/_admission/_lane_count/gap_census,"The dispatcher's one scheduling decision (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4/§A8), consuming schedule.kind_of - the §A1 classification's enforcement point. _kind_action is the ruled §A8 table verbatim per kind x human-held answer (`agent_common.human_holds`), and a human-held attestation/gate row SURFACES even under keep_nondependent — that dial answers whether OTHER lanes keep running, never whether the machine may ratify; _admission is the barrier as a pure decision: any exclusive-kind frontier row stops new admission (wait) until every lane is back, then ALL spine rows admit together as one exclusive batch (one branch, one agent_loop --wi 'A;B' worker, one re-attest window), attestation/gate rows surface under attended (drain, exit 0 naming the open-items cards) and admit as the queued batch at single-ratify's close; _lane_count resolves the lanes dial CLI > AGENT_LANES > stack.ini [agent-loop] > 1 (absent key = 1; the template seeds 2); gap_census is the empty-frontier ladder's rung-1 seam - unverified in-scope SRs, orphan rows and draft SNs from trace.analyze, handed to the intake mint helper (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2), never minted in the lane.","The §A4.1 authority flip: admission replaced the claim rung's deleted safety_class refusal, so the policy must live at ONE decision point and be driven as a pure function.",(see TC-143),Modified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-150,SR-132,"Lane mechanics: worktree, worker subprocess, §A2 refresh subprocess",project-trajectory/scripts/lane.py,ensure_worktree/worker_argv/spawn_worker/run_worker/spawn_refresh,"One lane's mechanics extracted from the dispatcher (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4.2): ensure the lane worktree (integrate.lane_worktree, the one home both the worker and the refresh agree on), build the worker argv with the plain launch's session dials forwarded explicitly, launch the worker subprocess (spawn_worker non-blocking for the lane table; run_worker its blocking form and the default worker seam), and run the §A2 station refresh as the lane's OWN subprocess (spawn_refresh - the invoker's integrate.py refresh CLI under the harness interpreter) so N lanes' bars overlap instead of queueing at the dispatcher (§A4.3). Reports outcomes, decides none: the tree signal (specs moved out of active/<branch>/) plus exit codes are the whole protocol - no state file, no back-channel.","The §A4.2 split keeps the deciding half (dispatch.py) apart from the running half, so at lanes=1 the dispatcher degenerates to the serial loop and the concurrency is a table size, not a second code path.",(see TC-144),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-151,SR-132,Dispatch-lock claim rung + the spine batch claim,project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py,_dispatch_lock/claim/_claim_refusal/_abandoned_claim,"The §A4.1 authority flip's integrator half (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4.1): _claim_refusal's safety_class != ordinary arm is DELETED (admission is the dispatcher's decision) and the claim REQUIRES the dispatch lock (agent_common.dispatch_lock_path, out/agent-loop.lock - the lock a live dispatcher holds for its process lifetime), acquired at ONE site (_dispatch_lock, mirroring _slot's one-site discipline for the merge slot's own lock) with the ladder run before it so the clean-trunk rung never refuses over the lock's own file: a hand claim on an idle station works, a hand claim during live lanes is unrepresentable, and the dispatcher's in-process claim passes dispatch_lock_held=True. claim/_claim_refusal/_abandoned_claim/_claim_delta are batch-aware: a spine batch is ONE claim commit moving every batched spec (subject joins the ids; the single-WI subject stays byte-identical), and integrate() takes an optional branches= restriction so the dispatcher merges each lane's branch as its own refresh completes.","A constraint instead of a check: the state the old refusal guarded against (a claim written beside live lanes) is now unrepresentable, and the batch makes N spine changes cost one re-attest window (§A4).",(see TC-145),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-152,SR-093,The adjudication kind: rank-1 slot + no-bar arm + the bar strictness key,project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py,SAFETY_CLASSES/_KIND_CONCURRENCY/_KIND_RANK,"The `adjudication` kind (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2) joins the declared SafetyClass vocabulary: exclusive on the concurrency axis, rank 1 on the order axis - the slot §A1 reserved, filled without renumbering the ruled table. The kind's lane runs NO product bar (integrate._lane_bar_directives + _refresh_bar: the refresh still merges trunk in, runs the trunk step and commits a verified Bar-Green attestation whose summary reads no-bar honestly) because its outputs are Status cells and the work registry, nothing a product bar can speak to; a mixed batch or unreadable frontmatter fails TOWARD the bar. The optional `bar = G1|G2|G3` frontmatter key rides the same directive read: bar declares verification strictness for this row's lane; it never affects scheduling (load_wis deliberately does not parse it) - the refresh passes the batch's strictest value to check.py --gate and refuses a malformed one loudly.",Narrowing the amendment detector to ratified cells without the kind would move mis-fires from spurious window to MISSED window; the two are a pair (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2).,(see TC-146),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-153,SR-132,The unified trunk-side intake mint + the context block + the ratification-level arms,project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py,intake_after_merge/mint_gap_rows/parse_dispositions/next_wi_id/context_block/flip_verified,"The R1/R3 invariant sealed (log.md Decisions 2026-08-01): a WI id is created only by a human trunk commit or this helper - lanes never mint. next_wi_id is max+1 over every spec FILENAME under docs/work/ (active/<branch>/ included). Three triggers, serial by construction: (a) the ratified/routed-cell diff on the merged commit via check_trajectory.staged_spine_amendments -> one adjudication row listing each changed row/cell/before-after (routed traced cells: SN-Refs, Verifies, and LLR SR-Refs per the ruled cell split); (b) a merged spec carrying ## Handback -> the disposition row (outcomes cancel / defer / re-queue with drafted follow-up / surface an open item; NEVER minted for an adjudication row - no recursion, and handback.hand_back refuses the act itself); (c) the dispatcher's gap census -> concrete gap-closure rows, deduped against every existing row. Drafts-not-mints: a merged adjudication row's ## Dispositions fenced-toml drafts mint at ITS merge, validated loudly. Tier signals are measurable (rows touched, gate delta, handback reason class). context_block renders the pure registry joins (cancelled precedent WITH REASONS first, pending OIs, the LLR/TC code map, CMP knowledge packs, IF seams, precedent reviews) - advisory-never-gating, three consumers. flip_verified enacts ruled decision 2: recommend-only under attended, flip under single-ratify/autonomous, unknown levels fail toward recommend. The mint commit mirrors the claim's bookkeeping shape; every derived title is deterministic, so the sweep CLI re-run is idempotent.","A detected event must FORCE a row into the registry with nobody watching, and an id two lanes could pick at once must stay unrepresentable (R1).",(see TC-147),Modified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-154,SR-132,The merge slot's post-merge intake arm,project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py,integrate_one/_lane_bar_directives/_refresh_bar,"integrate_one's post-merge arm is the intake's one honest hook point: the merge has landed, the slot is still held, trunk is serial - intake.intake_after_merge(root, pre-merge head, head, outcomes, branch) mints there as one bookkeeping commit (RULING-6: docs/work/ + the declared generated set), and a mint refusal stops the run loudly while the merge stands (recovery: fix trunk-side, then python intake.py sweep). The refresh's bar directives (_lane_bar_directives, read off the same trunk claim the slot reads) carry the adjudication no-bar arm and the bar strictness pin to _run_bar's --gate.",Minting anywhere else re-opens the two-lanes-one-id collision the trunk-only ruling ended; the slot is the only place trunk moves under a held lock.,(see TC-148),Verified,CMP-004,4
-LLR-155,SR-137;SR-139,"The config layer: one reader, checked shape, one comparison",project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py,process_config/process_shape_findings/config_conflicts/ratification_level/human_holds,"`process_config` parses `docs/process.toml` once; `declared_policy` reads a dial through it with the legacy file as fallback; `process_shape_findings` refuses every line shape the hooks' sh cannot follow; `config_conflicts` returns refusal STRINGS (never raises — `dispatch.run` sits in the tick loop's caller, where an exception would rewrite an exit-code contract) covering the double-declaration, the wrong type and the out-of-range value; `ratification_level` + `human_holds` are the ordinal and the ONE comparison every consumer makes.","Five entry points read policy without passing through `agent_loop.main`, so the refusal has to live where the value is read. The comparison is stated once for the same reason the shape is checked: the failure modes here are silent, and a rule restated at four call sites is four chances to state it differently.",TC-150;TC-151,Draft,CMP-002,5
-LLR-156,SR-138,"The legacy converter, and the one word that is not a rename",project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py,migrate_legacy_config/_migrate_gate_policy/set_process_key/LEGACY_RATIFICATION,"`set_process_key` answers THREE states (`wrote`/`unchanged`/`missing`) and the write decides the delete; `add_if_missing` makes the write total. `gate-policy` gets its own arm because its single word expands to three dials, so it cannot be a one-key `LEGACY_CONFIG` row. `LEGACY_RATIFICATION` is F5-duplicated from `agent_common` (bootstrap imports no kit sibling — it must run standing on nothing but the kit directory it copies FROM) and pinned equal by `test_rule_sync`.","This is duplicated POLICY, not plumbing: a migrator and a reader disagreeing about what `single-ratify` meant would let a repo scaffold with one posture and run with another — which is the shadowing defect the ordinal was introduced to remove, returning through the migration path.",TC-152,Draft,CMP-002,5
-LLR-157,SR-139,The second axis: which tier is in process,project-trajectory/scripts/derive_gate.py,spine_stage/stage_to_gate/_decomposed_sr_ids,"`spine_stage` returns the LOWEST unfinished tier, where a MISSING artifact belongs to its own tier rather than to its parent (an SR with no LLR yet is stage 2, because what is being written is an LLR), `Modified` is the SR's own tier and is checked FIRST, and any other not-yet-Verified SR is checked LAST because a child in flight is the honest answer while it is in flight. `stage_to_gate` states the mapping to the harness gate once, deliberately lossy in one direction only. The value rides `docs/gate` as an APPENDED basis field.","Reading a missing child as the parent's tier made stages 2 and 3 unreachable during exactly the period they describe, leaving the axis unable to express the distinction it exists for. Appending rather than inserting the basis field is required: `check.py`'s basis regex depends on the existing field order.",TC-151,Draft,CMP-002,5
-LLR-158,SR-140,The digest that anchors an attestation,project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py,normative_text/sn_normative_text/digest/current_digests,"normative_text() joins a row's RATIFIED cells in header order with a unit separator, reusing spine_cell_class rather than a second list so the digest and the amend-without-flip warn can never disagree about what normative means; the id and Status are excluded because neither is content - the id is the key the digest is looked up BY, and folding Status in would make every flip look like an amendment and every amendment invisible behind its own flip. sn_normative_text() takes the RAW table line for a stakeholder need rather than a parsed projection, because the three need tables have different shapes and a projection would hash the garbling. current_digests() is the one reader that joins both halves. The WRITER and the co-mutation guard are not here yet: which cells hold the anchor depends on the registry carrier, ruled separately (OI-12).","Integrity is hard because a ledger that does not parse is not an anchor at all and every reader silently falls back to the git-history derivation it replaces. Removal was the gap: it silenced all three rungs at once, so the strongest form of rewriting was the one form the guard could not see.",TC-153,Draft,CMP-001,5
-LLR-159,SR-141;SR-142,Admission order and the red-TC census,project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py,_judgement_first/_admission/red_tc_census/parse_red_tc/_implemented_ids,"`_judgement_first` is a STABLE PARTITION over the frontier, applied inside `_admission` before the surface/dispatch split so it can never move work past a human-held stop. `red_tc_census` rides the existing finding-string seam with a distinct prefix; `_red_tc_line` and `parse_red_tc` sit adjacent because the grammar is a contract between two modules, and `intake` routes on the parser rather than re-splitting prose.","The alternative to the partition — renumbering `schedule._KIND_RANK` — would change ordering for every downstream repo for a rung that belongs to admission. The alternative to the parser is prose carrying control flow, which is the exact defect the typed `suggested_tier` field replaced.",TC-154,Draft,CMP-004,5
-LLR-160,SR-143,The mechanical queue-overlap pre-filter,project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py,queue_conflict_findings/_title_tokens,"Three signals over OPEN rows only — Jaccard over title token sets at a deliberately high bar, shared `SR-Refs`, and a shared anchor-stripped `SpecRef` — each reported once per pair per signal with both ids, in sorted order. Printed on the warn-only line beside backlog staleness; never appended to `errors`.","A low similarity bar turns a useful warn into noise that gets ignored, which is worse than not having the warn: this rung catches the same job minted twice, not two rows in the same area.",TC-155,Draft,CMP-001,5
-LLR-161,SR-144;SR-145,The close ritual and the disposition it owes,project-trajectory/scripts/handback.py,close_partial/render_report/report_path/_restore/_split_lines,"`close_partial` writes the report FIRST (it is the event's identity), refuses when one already exists, and restores EVERY report this call wrote on any refusal — so a multi-spec lane cannot half-close. `intake._close_drafts` titles the disposition on the report PATH and nothing else, and says so LOUDLY when a terminal spec has no report rather than minting zero rows quietly.","The path is the only token in the event's identity that cannot move. An earlier cut folded the report's `claimed_outcome` in as well, which re-read a MUTABLE field on every sweep, so one edit minted a second disposition for the same close — the starvation class returning through a new proxy.",TC-156,Draft,CMP-003,5
-LLR-162,SR-146,"Prompts as loaded files, catalogued and fingerprinted",project-trajectory/scripts/prompts.py,load/fill/strict_check/digest/catalog_rows/preflight,"`load` reads a shipped template (or an operator override) and raises a named `PromptError`; `fill` refuses an unknown slot and an unfilled declared slot; `digest` normalizes line endings before hashing; `preflight` turns a missing template into a launch-time refusal. `gen_prompt_catalog.py` renders the catalogue with a `--check` freshness gate, and `agent_common.prompt_fingerprint` records the rendered result per session beside the template's own name.","Normalizing before hashing is not cosmetic: the same template checked out CRLF on Windows and LF on POSIX is the same prompt, and a telemetry field that said otherwise would report a model change on every clone.",TC-157,Draft,CMP-002,5
-LLR-163,SR-146,"Argv arrays, and the adjudicator as a routed phase",project-trajectory/scripts/agent_session.py,split_cmd/build_argv,"`split_cmd` recognises a JSON array ONLY when the value parses as a list, so no existing template can change meaning; a non-string token raises rather than being stringified. The prompt-transport decision reads the resulting TOKENS, so it is identical for both forms. Alongside it, `ADJUDICATE` joins `agent_route.WEIGHT_PHASES` and `agent_loop.DEFAULT_PHASE_TIER` with its own `route_intent` arm: cross-family like the reviewers, tier pinned by the row's measured estimate, and deliberately deaf to the implementer's escalation overrides.",A silent coercion in a registry is a launch that does not match its declaration. And an adjudicator inheriting the implementer's escalation would silently re-tier a judgement because a different row's build went badly.,TC-157,Draft,CMP-002,5
-LLR-164,SR-146,The generated prompt catalogue and its freshness gate,project-trajectory/scripts/gen_prompt_catalog.py,render/main,"`render` is PURE — it takes no arguments and reads only the shipped templates — so `--check` and the write path cannot disagree about what ""current"" means. `--check` compares on NORMALIZED line endings, for the same reason the digest is taken that way: a CRLF checkout is not a stale catalogue. The file is declared in `docs/stack.ini [generated]` as kind `promptcatalog`, so the trunk lane owns it and a work branch never commits it.","A generated artifact nobody re-generates is a document that lies, and a lying catalogue is worse than none: it invites the operator to conclude the wrong thing about the session they are debugging. Same contract `gen_arch_map --check` and `gen_skills_index --check` hold.",TC-157,Draft,CMP-002,5
-LLR-165,SR-147,The spine carrier converter (markdown + CSV -> TOML),project-trajectory/scripts/migrate_carrier.py,convert/rows_to_toml/sn_to_toml/compare/toml_scalar,"convert() walks the four tiers and returns (findings, written_paths), so the caller decides whether a lossy conversion writes. rows_to_toml emits `[<table>.<ID>]` with the id PREFIX RETAINED and BARE - TOML allows `-` in a bare key - because ~6,400 hand-authored citations use the prefixed token and a numeric key buys no type change (TOML keys are always strings, and `001` and `1` are two legal DIFFERENT keys, which is a near-miss the prefixed form does not have). cell_to_value types by COLUMN, not by guessing: REF_COLS become arrays, INT_COLS become ints, an empty cell becomes an absent key, and everything else stays a string - this is a carrier change, not a schema change. toml_scalar emits multi-line basic strings for anything long or newline-bearing (tomllib is read-only by design, so the kit emits its own, as it already does in wi_convert.toml_string and bootstrap.set_process_key). sn_to_toml keeps the edge-case tier's own lifecycle/scenario/expected fields as THEMSELVES and writes kind= from the heading, retiring section-as-state. compare() re-reads what was emitted and diffs it against an {id: {key: text}} projection built from either carrier, so ONE comparison serves both instead of a second copy that drifts.","The round-trip check is the requirement, not a convenience - and a check that cannot fail would be the false green SN-008 forbids, which is why the loss detector is driven against real corruption classes rather than only against a clean run. Ref cells compare on the TOKEN LIST because the conversion re-joins them with the canonical separator and the original spacing was never part of the meaning; comparing raw text there would report a loss that is not one. The edge-case fold that traj_parse._sn_fields performs is a PRESENTATION rule the markdown table forced, so it stays in the exporters - baking it into the carrier would make the exporter's reading the only reading there is.",TC-159,Draft,CMP-005,5
-LLR-166,SR-147,"The spine carrier's one reader, and the dual-home refusal",project-trajectory/scripts/spine_carrier.py,SPINE_TABLE/SPINE_COLUMN/rows_from_toml/rows_from_text/resolve/load,"PURE like trace_text (text in, rows out; no git, no argv) except resolve()'s existence check, because the dual-home refusal is the one rule that must not be duplicated. rows_from_toml presents rows under TODAY'S COLUMN NAMES via SPINE_COLUMN - the exact inverse of migrate_carrier.KEY - so the carrier change is data plus a loader rather than a sixteen-module rename; SPINE_TABLE is keyed on the ID COLUMN, not on a path, because a path carries a carrier suffix and therefore moves. An absent key stays absent rather than becoming """", and a decode error returns None rather than {}. resolve() RAISES when both carriers exist rather than picking by precedence, and load() raises on a carrier that exists and will not parse. carriers() returns BOTH paths so an applicability test spans the cutover.","None-vs-{} and absent-vs-empty are the same defect twice: {} says the registry had no rows, which for a baseline read means re-bless everything with no diff and for a gate means nothing to check, so a carrier that cannot be read must be ABSENT rather than empty. The dual-home REFUSAL follows the rule bootstrap --migrate-config already applies to the policy dials: a precedence rule lets a half-finished migration keep working while quietly reading the stale half, so the conversion is never finished and nobody finds out. ONE HOME for the vocabulary is an owner ruling (2026-08-10, repo-lock D-6) amending F5: duplicating a vocabulary fails silently - the copy that has not learned a column returns a row missing that cell, which every consumer reads as ""the cell is empty"".",TC-160,Draft,CMP-001,5
