-SR-121,Superseded: Gated downstream migration to the parallel default,SN-025;SN-012,"SR-121 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.","Identity history for the Phase 5 deletion (the SS6 fate table): the requirement's job survives in the integration seam, its machinery does not.",The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.,,S,Inspection,Verified,4,Parallel dispatch,SR-132
-SR-122,Dashboard freshness check,SN-010;SN-021,"gen_trajectory.py --check shall fail on stale or missing output, the visible git-derived as-of stamp being excluded from stamp-only comparisons.","Realizes SN-021 — a committed dashboard that nobody re-checks becomes a confident picture of a project that has moved on, and the as-of stamp changes on every commit, so comparing it would make the check cry wolf and get disabled. Regenerating on read was rejected: the artifact is committed so it can be read without the toolchain.","Stale or missing output fails --check, and as-of behavior is stable in and outside Git.",,M,Test,Verified,1,Trajectory,
-SR-123,Critique closure ratchet,SN-024;SN-006,check_trajectory.py shall warn when a Critique WI closes after CHANGES-REQUESTED without validation evidence changing.,"Realizes SN-006 — a changes-requested verdict followed by a close with nothing altered is how a critique becomes a formality, and nothing else in the harness can see that pattern. Hard-failing the close was rejected: the evidence sometimes lands outside the registries, so the check reports rather than adjudicates.","The lax-closure case warns, while an approval or a TC, test or rubric delta stays silent.",,S,Test,Verified,1,Unattended loop,
-SR-124,Contradiction-safe dual-plan dispatcher class,SN-024;SN-006,schedule.py shall derive a contradiction-safe single-WI scheduling class from PlanMode.,"Realizes SN-024 — a row whose PlanMode and SafetyClass disagree has no correct lane, and guessing one dispatches contradictory work rather than surfacing the contradiction. Defaulting to the safer class was rejected: it hides a registry error that only a human can resolve.","PlanMode derives a single-WI class, and a contradictory SafetyClass quarantines the row.",,S,Test,Verified,4,Dual-plan decomposition,
-SR-125,PAGE maps through gate policy,SN-024;SN-006,"The --dual-plan single-shot round shall map a PAGE through the declared ratification level, hard-gating a human only while the tier in process is still HUMAN-HELD and otherwise reaching the pause-free attention (STALL) end state.","Realizes SN-016 — a PAGE that always stops for a human makes the unattended loop attended in practice, while one that never stops discards the judgement the round asked for. Two separate mappings for the dispatcher and the single-shot round were rejected: they drift, and the drift shows up as a loop that hangs on one path only.","A PAGE preserves evidence and follows the declared level (a human-held tier -> NEEDS-HUMAN exit; a loop-held tier -> STALL exit, no human gate); an unreadable level or stage resolves to human-held, so the failure direction is the gate, never past it.",,S,Test,Modified,4,Dual-plan decomposition,
-SR-126,Spine stand-alone rule,SN-002;SN-010,trace.py shall fail under --strict when any spine row's normative text cites a work item or a process doc.,"Realizes SN-010 — a row that cites a work item is unreadable to the audience that matters most: a downstream adopter, or an agent, with none of this project's history, who must resolve an id they cannot see before they know what the system does. A process-doc citation fails differently and just as badly: a row that cites the rule it is obeying inverts the relationship, so amending the process silently changes what the row appears to require. Warning instead of failing was tried and rejected: a warn nobody must act on is how 43 such rows accumulated before anyone measured them.","A WI id or a process-doc citation in an SR Title/Requirement/Rationale/AcceptanceCriteria, an LLR Title/Detail/Rationale, or a TC Method/Expected/Parameters exits 1 under --strict and names the registry, row and cell; a script name, artifact path, rubric or sibling spine id does not; pointer columns are out of scope.",,M,Test,Verified,1,Traceability,
-SR-127,Requirement form: one testable obligation,SN-002;SN-004,"trace.py shall fail under --strict when a non-Draft requirement repeats its obligation keyword, uses a different modal in normative text, states an obligation with no actor, or uses an unfalsifiable or open-ended term.","Realizes SN-004 — when one row carries two obligations and only one holds, no id reports the failure, so the gate passes on a partially-met requirement. Undecidable wording fails differently and worse: an unfalsifiable adjective cannot be tested, so the row is signed on impression. Each rule is a LEXICAL proxy for a semantic property and is scoped to what it can actually decide — counting the obligation keyword catches the shape that recurred here and does not claim to find every multi-obligation row. A semantic check was rejected as out of reach for a stdlib checker, and a readability score was rejected as a proxy for the wrong property: it measures prose complexity, not whether a reader can tell what is binding.","A second `shall`, a non-`shall` modal, an actorless `shall be <participle>`, an unfalsifiable term, an open-ended escape clause, or a `shall` in an LLR Detail exits 1 under --strict; a Draft row, a zero-`shall` row, `must` as a project's own obligation keyword, and a multi-clause AcceptanceCriteria do not.",,M,Test,Verified,1,Traceability,
-SR-128,Paraphrase advisory,SN-002,"trace.py shall warn (never fail) when an SR Rationale re-words its own Requirement, or an LLR Detail re-words its parent SR Requirement, past a declared word-overlap threshold.","Realizes SN-002 — a child that restates its parent adds a second home for one fact and no decomposition, so the trace grows a level without gaining information and a later amendment updates one copy. It warns rather than fails because the detector is word overlap: a short, correct Detail legitimately shares vocabulary with the requirement it decomposes, and gating on a heuristic with a large legitimate majority produces a check that gets scrolled past. The two relationships named are the ones where the parent is unambiguous; a general child-cell rule was rejected because 'the parent' is not well defined for the rest.","An SR Rationale overlapping its own Requirement, or an LLR Detail overlapping its parent SR Requirement, past the declared threshold emits a stdout WARNING and a report line; the exit code is unchanged.",,S,Test,Verified,1,Traceability,
-SR-129,Registry representation migration,SN-002;SN-012,"The kit shall provide a mechanical converter between the work-item registry's two homes - the docs/work/ spec folder (status = directory, TOML frontmatter, Deliverable in the body) and the legacy docs/requirements/work-items.csv - refusing any status outside the known vocabulary, any materialization into a non-empty target, and any conversion while an in-flight active/<branch>/ claim exists (conversion is a drained-stop operation), with the round-trip proven over the live registry before an authority flip.","The 140-cell lesson: an unproven representation change is where a registry silently loses cells. The converter is the migration path every adopter of the folder home takes, and the single spec writer the filing seam reuses (concurrency-restructure spec, Phase 2).",wi_convert --verify over a populated registry exits 0 reporting cell-exact equality; an unknown status refuses naming the row; folder->CSV->folder is parse-equal including a hand-filed spec with no order key; an in-flight claim refuses conversion by name.,,C,Test,Verified,4,Trajectory,
-SR-130,Serial trunk step compiles log fragments and regenerates authority,SN-027;SN-008,"A serial trunk step shall compile docs/log.d/ work-branch log fragments into docs/log.md in merge order derived from git history - validating every fragment before any write, rebasing relative links to the log's home, deleting compiled fragments, failing loudly at the first error and never committing.","The §5.1/§5.2 shared-surface rules (concurrency-restructure): no work branch writes log.md, so hand-merged logs become unrepresentable; the one deliberately serial actor stays tiny, idempotent and fail-loud (§5.5). The artifact-regen half is SR-134's obligation (one row, one obligation).",Two committed fragments compile in git-add order with links rebased docs/-relative and the fragments deleted; an uncommitted or malformed fragment blocks the whole batch with nothing appended; a fragment-free run is a clean no-op.,,M,Test,Modified,4,Trajectory,
-SR-131,Tracked pause drains claiming to a merged stop,SN-027;SN-016,"A tracked docs/work/pause file (TOML: reason, since) shall pause claiming - everything in flight finishes and integrates - read via pause_reason as the ONE pause home (the legacy untracked docs/pause half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5), failing closed on malformation.","§5.6: pause means stop claiming and drain to a merged stop - never a strand. The tracked form survives clones, its reason is diffable history, and unpausing is an auditable deletion commit; a broken pause file must still pause, loudly. The pending-surface render is SR-135's obligation (one row, one obligation).",A tracked pause file pauses the coordinator via pause_reason under the unchanged None/''/reason contract; a malformed file pauses with the pinned loud placeholder; a stray legacy docs/pause file is inert.,,M,Test,Modified,4,Trajectory,
-SR-132,Local integrator: serial fail-closed merge queue,SN-027;SN-008,"The local integrator shall claim work via a serial trunk commit (queued spec to docs/work/active/<branch>/, branch cut from that commit, refused while docs/work/pause is present), merge each finished claimed branch --no-ff onto a candidate worktree with the trunk step folded into the merge commit, run the declared bar on the composed tree - refusing on a missing or empty check declaration and on any SKIP in the bar's own report - require the policy-dialed verdict artifacts with git-derived freshness, fast-forward the trunk only on green, and stop loudly on red.",The §1.2 seam's default backend: serial => composed-tree gating by construction; fail-closed against the dispatcher's recorded fail-open (a skipped bar merged 8 trains with zero gate-verified). Verdict freshness is git-derived because the WI-scoped §5.4 naming dropped the sha7 filename binding - without it a stale APPROVE clears the gate.,"A claim moves the spec and cuts the branch in one serial trunk step and is refused when paused, dirty, name-unsafe, not-ordinary or not-ready; a finished branch is detected from its tree alone; an absent or stale or CHANGES-REQUESTED verdict refuses the merge; a missing stack.ini or empty [product] test refuses; a red bar parks the candidate and stops the queue; the RULING-6 window audit flags a non-merge product commit.",,M,Test,Modified,4,Trajectory,
-SR-133,Work-branch lane skip for freshness steps,SN-027;SN-008,On a claimed work branch (one whose history holds a docs/work/active/<branch>/ claim) check.py's generated-artifact freshness steps shall SKIP with a stated trunk-lane reason - generated artifacts are trunk-only - while every non-freshness step still runs.,"The skip is not a weakening: a work branch cannot commit generated artifacts at all (concurrency-restructure §5.2), so demanding their freshness there would force the very conflict the rule deletes; the skip is reported, never silent, and any signal ambiguity resolves toward the full trunk bar. Decomposed from SR-006 so each row states one obligation.","on a claimed work branch the declared freshness steps SKIP with a §5.2 notice and exit code untouched while skills-sync, trajectory and registry-integrity still run; the closing commit and its successors keep the skip (the signal derives from branch history, not the working tree); the trunk lane never skips them.",,M,Test,Modified,4,Gate harness,
-SR-134,Trunk step regenerates the declared artifacts in dependency order,SN-027;SN-008,The serial trunk step shall regenerate the declared generated artifacts in dependency order - the derived gate before its consumers - failing loudly at the first error and never committing.,"Stale-artifact merge conflicts become unrepresentable when only the serial trunk actor regenerates (§5.2/§5.5); dependency order because docs/gate is an input to the dashboard and status views, not an output. Decomposed from SR-130 so each row states one obligation.",--regen skips absent artifact families with a notice and stops loudly at the first failing generator; derive_gate runs before the dashboard/status/open-items regens.,,M,Test,Modified,4,Trajectory,
-SR-135,Pending surface renders the declared pause verbatim,SN-027;SN-016,The generated pending surface shall render a declared pause's since and reason verbatim - no clock - clearing when the pause file is deleted.,"An open pause is a visible accruing cost, never a forgotten one (the stale-reason lesson). Decomposed from SR-131 so each row states one obligation.","the pending block renders Paused since <date> - <reason> with the declared date verbatim (no clock), omits the stamp when since is empty, renders the pinned fail-closed placeholder on malformation, and clears when the file is deleted.",,M,Test,Modified,4,Trajectory,
-SR-136,Declared-figure provenance,SN-008;SN-010,"check_figures.py shall flag a declared driven figure - a line opting in under the fig: marker convention - that does not carry both the command that produced it and the revision it was driven at, or a derivation naming the declared figures it is computed from, warn-first (exit 0) with --strict gating; presence only, never truth, and an unmarked figure is out of scope by design.","Realizes SN-008 and SN-010 - a driven figure is evidence only at the revision it was driven on, and three false figures in one session (2026-08-01) each cost a review round because no record named the producing command or revision, so a stale figure read exactly like a live one. Digit detection was rejected (ids, dates, section numbers and byte budgets drown it, and one false figure had no digits at all), so the marker is opt-in - declare, don't infer - making the honest claim 'declared figures carry provenance', never 'all figures do'. Re-derivation is rung 2, deliberately not built: recorded commands are an execution surface, most figures are legitimately historical, and some commands are expensive or non-deterministic.","A fig: marker missing cmd= or rev= (or carrying only empty or wordless values) warns naming the missing half and gates only under --strict, each marker on a line judged on its own attributes; cmd= plus rev= (bare or quoted), or a non-empty derived=, passes; unmarked figure prose, placeholder-grammar examples, fig-ok lines, GENERATED blocks and docs/reviews/ records are ignored; docs/stack.ini is scanned beside the root and docs markdown surfaces.",,S,Test,Verified,1,Doc currency,
-SR-137,"One policy home, with a checked shape",SN-028,"The kit shall read every process policy dial from a single `docs/process.toml`, and shall REFUSE — never resolve by precedence — a repo in which any dial is declared both there and in its legacy one-word file. The file's line shape shall be a checked contract: one `key = value` per line under a bare `[section]` header, no dotted keys, no inline tables, no multi-line strings.","Two grammars read this file — `tomllib` in Python and the three git hooks in pure sh, so a Python-less box still fails closed on a declared privacy gate (M-42). TOML is far more expressive than a `grep -E` can follow, so every shape only one reader understands is a silent flip of a security gate; the first cut shipped five such shapes, three of them fail-OPEN. Narrowing the shape and CHECKING the narrowing is what makes one file safe for two readers.","A dial declared in both homes produces a refusal naming the key and both files, at every guarded entry point (the dispatcher's pre-claim preflight, intake's adjudication arm, the integrator's verdict gate); a dotted key, inline table, multi-line string or hook-read key under the wrong section is refused by the shape check AND still reads ON in both readers; the hooks' sh and `tomllib` return the same answer over the adversarial file table; a wrong-typed or out-of-range dial is refused rather than defaulted.",,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-138,Legacy config converts automatically and totally,SN-028,"`bootstrap.py --migrate-config` shall fold every legacy one-word policy file into `docs/process.toml` and delete it, and shall NEVER delete a file whose value it did not manage to write. Both bootstrap and the documented re-sync shall run it.","The hard mixed-config refusal in SR-137 is only safe to ship if a downstream adopter never meets it un-aided. And the converter's own failure mode is the sharp one: an earlier cut deleted a legacy file on the strength of a write that never happened and reported success, destroying a declared `privacy_check = true` under a green `migrated:` line.",A repo carrying legacy files converts in one pass and reports what moved; a second pass is a no-op; a legacy file whose value cannot be written is LEFT IN PLACE with its value intact and named in the report; the retired gate-authority word expands to all three dials it meant rather than to a single key.,,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-139,Ratification as an ordinal over a derived spine stage,SN-029,"The kit shall express human ratification authority as a cumulative integer 0-4 naming the highest spine tier a human still ratifies, compared against a separately derived SPINE STAGE (0=SN..4=nothing in process) with a declared, auditable mapping to the harness gate. Every unreadable or out-of-range input shall resolve toward MORE human involvement.","The retired three-value enum could not express ""TCs are human-held but LLRs are not"", and four tables each re-interpreted the same word with its own fail-safe direction. The failure that matters is a machine ratifying something a human meant to hold, so every degrade has to point the same way — including the ones that look like kindness: clamping an out-of-range level toward 0 reads as ""nothing is human-held"" and disarms every hold in the repo.","Level N holds exactly the tiers the shipped template documents (cumulative, strictly-less-than the stage); stage 4 is held by no level, the end-of-run hold being its own dial; an unreadable stage, an absent dial, and a wrong-typed dial all read as human-held; an out-of-range level falls back to the conservative end AND is refused loudly; a legacy gate-policy word reads as all three dials it meant.",,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-140,The attestation anchor lives on the artifact's own row,SN-029,"The kit shall record each acceptance ON THE ACCEPTED ARTIFACT'S OWN ROW - the commit whose tree carries the text that was accepted, and a digest of that row's NORMATIVE cells - never in a second registry keyed on the same artifact; shall report text that has moved away from its recorded anchor regardless of any Status movement; and shall treat an anchor written in the same commit as the text it anchors as an error.","Attestation STATE is already the row's own Status - derive_gate computes the gate from those cells and reads no other source - so a separate ledger was a second home for a fact the row already carried, and three of its seven columns duplicated cells outright (Decision=ratified|meaning IS Status; superseded IS SupersededBy). What the ledger got right is the ANCHOR ITSELF: 'has this been ratified' was answered by walking git for the newest commit where a row read Verified, which is sound only while every amendment flips its row in the same commit - precisely the sanctioned path the staged-amendment guard deliberately ignores. Two cells are kept rather than one because they fail in DIFFERENT directions: the commit is what a human can review and what the before/after brief diffs against, while the digest is what survives a squash-merge, a rebase or a shallow clone, any of which makes a recorded sha unresolvable and would otherwise silence drift detection repo-wide at once. Owner ruling 2026-08-09, docs/repo-lock.md D-1.","An artifact whose normative text has moved since its recorded anchor is reported whether or not its Status moved; an anchor that cannot be recomputed, or that names an artifact which is not a current row, is a finding rather than silence; a commit that writes an anchor cell while also changing that row's normative cells is refused, because a re-stamp in the same breath as an amendment hides the amendment; and the same rules hold for a stakeholder need, which has no Status cell for the amendment machinery to watch.",,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-141,The loop's priority order is stated and pinned,SN-025,"Admission shall prefer `adjudication` rows over all other ready work, as a stable partition applied at admission rather than by renumbering the ruled rank table, and shall not thereby admit anything past a human-held stop.","A ratification is the one act this loop cannot cheaply take back — the ledger is append-only by design — so a spine batch that re-attests requirements must not run ahead of an unjudged claim that may overturn its premise. Renumbering the ruled rank table would migrate every downstream repo's ordering for a rung that belongs to admission, so the two orders are kept distinct and the disagreement is stated.","An adjudication row admits ahead of a ready spine batch; the ruled rank table is unchanged, so `--explain` still reports it; the partition is stable (non-adjudication rows keep their relative order); a human-held attestation still drains and surfaces with the adjudication row waiting behind the stop.",,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-142,Red test cases under a closed implementation are named and judged,SN-025,"The idle census shall name, as its own class, a test case that is not green while every requirement it verifies is cited by a work item recorded `done`, and shall route it to an ADJUDICATION row whose tier is estimated from measured breadth rather than defaulted.","The pair is a contradiction the registries state plainly and nothing was reading: the work is closed and the evidence for it is red. It is not one situation but three — a stale TC, an optimistic close, or a moved requirement — and choosing between them is a judgement, which is what the adjudication kind is for. The statuses that are NOT red are stated as exemptions because Status is an open vocabulary: failing toward naming a gap beats failing toward missing one.","A not-green TC under a `done` citer is named; `Verified`, `Draft` and `Modified` are exempt (the last belongs to the amendment adjudication, and two judgement rows for one event is a double-count); an open, `partial` or `cancelled` citer makes nothing red; a TC verifying a mix of claimed and unclaimed targets is not red; the census line's grammar has exactly one reader.",,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-143,"Queue overlap is made visible, never blocked",SN-025,"The validator shall report, warn-only and never in the exit code, pairs of OPEN work items that overlap by near-identical title, shared requirement citation, or a shared spec of record.","A queue that silently grows two rows for one job is the failure; a checker that cannot tell a legitimate split from a duplicate must not block on the difference. Overlap is frequently correct — two rows may answer one requirement from different sides — so the whole contribution of the mechanical rung is visibility, and the judgement half belongs to an adjudicator with the scope in front of it.","Each overlapping OPEN pair is named once per signal with both ids, in deterministic order; a closed row never conflicts; the title bar is high enough that ordinary unrelated work is silent and a shared stopword is not a signal; the findings never join the exit code, not even under --strict.",,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-144,Every lane close is a terminal state with an immutable record,SN-027,"A lane that cannot finish shall close into a TERMINAL `partial/` state with one immutable per-close report naming the claimed outcome, the reason, the commit range and the keep/discard split, and shall merge like any other branch. A report declaring neither a split nor an explicit deferral shall be refused, and a second close of the same event shall be refused rather than overwriting the first.","Five successive dedup mechanisms leaked because each reconstructed the return event from a MUTABLE proxy; a document that never moves dissolves the class instead of mitigating it. The refusals are what make ""immutable"" a property rather than a claim. The split is a field because its absence merged rejected code onto trunk on 2026-08-03: silence about it is different from saying ""I could not judge"", and only silence is unactionable.","A partial close is clean under the real validator (the terminal Deliverable rule is exempt, because an early close leaves the spec's definition byte-identical by design); a terminal spec with no report is LOUD rather than silently un-disposed; a second close is refused and leaves no residue in the lane; a multi-spec close that refuses restores every report it wrote.",,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-145,A lane's outcome is a claim; the adjudicator overrules by minting,SN-027,"Every `partial` and `cancelled` close shall earn one disposition row, keyed on the close's own immutable report path; a `complete` close shall be spot-checked at a declared rate rather than gated. Continuing partial work shall mean DRAFTING A SUCCESSOR carrying explicit lineage, never re-opening a terminal row.","The lane is entitled to assert what happened; what it MEANS is the adjudicator's, and making that authoritative by minting keeps history honest — the original move is never reversed in place. Keying the disposition on the report PATH rather than on any field of it is what makes re-sweep idempotence correct: an earlier cut keyed on the report's mutable outcome field and re-minted on every edit. Adjudicating every green close would rebuild the verdict gate under a new name, over work two gates already judged.",A partial or cancelled close mints exactly one disposition; a re-sweep mints none; a genuinely second close is a second report and so a second row; a disposition row never mints a disposition for itself (refused at both ends); a successor's lineage survives the id change.,,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-146,Prompts are reviewable files with a per-session audit trail,SN-005,"Every prompt the loop launches shall be a shipped file with strictly filled slots, listed by digest in a freshness-gated generated catalogue, with each session recording which template it used and the fingerprint of what that rendered to.","Prose steers the sessions this loop launches and had been reviewable only by reading Python source, which makes the process trusted rather than inspectable. The audit trail is the part that makes the move useful rather than cosmetic: without a per-session digest, ""which instruction did this session see"" stays an inference. (The argv-vs-shell-string transport decision is design, and lives one tier down in LLR-163.)",An unknown or unfilled slot is a refusal; a missing shipped template is a named preflight refusal rather than a first-session crash; the catalogue lists every shipped prompt with its digest and `--check` fails when stale; a line-ending difference is not a digest change; an operator override is named in the session record rather than only in the launch flags.,,S,Test,Draft,5,Process,
-SR-147,One machine-parseable carrier for the requirement spine,SN-002;SN-012,"The kit shall carry the SN->SR->LLR->TC spine in ONE machine-parseable carrier rather than two (stakeholder needs as markdown prose tables, the other three tiers as CSV), and shall provide a mechanical converter that proves the conversion cell-for-cell over the live registries BEFORE the authority flip - refusing, with a nonzero exit that names the row and the field, any conversion in which a cell does not survive the round-trip.","The two-carrier split has no recorded rationale and costs on both sides. Reading 32 need rows takes ~166 code lines across 14 functions in 8 modules - six bespoke scanners, two of them F5 twins pinned by nothing but a docstring, which have already drifted once and rendered a phantom root in the dashboard - while reading all 436 SR+LLR+TC rows takes csv.DictReader. CSV also cannot represent the cells that actually exist: five hold a literal pipe, 552 hold commas, the longest is 1,553 characters, and an embedded newline has no representation at all. A structured carrier turns three integrity rules into properties of the parse - a duplicate id becomes a decode error, a ref list becomes a typed array (retiring the split-on-whitespace rule that read `and` as an orphan), and an empty cell becomes an absent key, so `unset` and `set to empty` stop being the same value. The converter is the migration's evidence, not its convenience: SR-129's 140-cell lesson is that an unproven representation change is exactly where a registry silently loses cells.","migrate_carrier.py --check over the live registries exits 0 reporting the round-trip clean for every row of every tier; a cell that is dropped, truncated, re-typed or re-ordered is REFUSED with exit 1 and a finding naming the registry, the row id and the field; and the emitted text re-parses with tomllib.",,C,Test,Draft,5,Trajectory,
