+[requirement.SR-141]
+title = "The loop's priority order is stated and pinned"
+sn_refs = ["SN-025"]
+requirement = """Admission shall prefer `adjudication` rows over all other ready work, as a stable partition applied at admission rather than by renumbering the ruled rank table, and shall not thereby admit anything past a human-held stop."""
+rationale = """A ratification is the one act this loop cannot cheaply take back — the ledger is append-only by design — so a spine batch that re-attests requirements must not run ahead of an unjudged claim that may overturn its premise. Renumbering the ruled rank table would migrate every downstream repo's ordering for a rung that belongs to admission, so the two orders are kept distinct and the disagreement is stated."""
+acceptance_criteria = """An adjudication row admits ahead of a ready spine batch; the ruled rank table is unchanged, so `--explain` still reports it; the partition is stable (non-adjudication rows keep their relative order); a human-held attestation still drains and surfaces with the adjudication row waiting behind the stop."""
+priority = "S"
+verification = "Test"
+status = "Draft"
+phase = 5
+area = "Process"
+
+[requirement.SR-142]
+title = "Red test cases under a closed implementation are named and judged"
+sn_refs = ["SN-025"]
+requirement = """The idle census shall name, as its own class, a test case that is not green while every requirement it verifies is cited by a work item recorded `done`, and shall route it to an ADJUDICATION row whose tier is estimated from measured breadth rather than defaulted."""
+rationale = """The pair is a contradiction the registries state plainly and nothing was reading: the work is closed and the evidence for it is red. It is not one situation but three — a stale TC, an optimistic close, or a moved requirement — and choosing between them is a judgement, which is what the adjudication kind is for. The statuses that are NOT red are stated as exemptions because Status is an open vocabulary: failing toward naming a gap beats failing toward missing one."""
+acceptance_criteria = """A not-green TC under a `done` citer is named; `Verified`, `Draft` and `Modified` are exempt (the last belongs to the amendment adjudication, and two judgement rows for one event is a double-count); an open, `partial` or `cancelled` citer makes nothing red; a TC verifying a mix of claimed and unclaimed targets is not red; the census line's grammar has exactly one reader."""
+priority = "S"
+verification = "Test"
+status = "Draft"
+phase = 5
+area = "Process"
+
+[requirement.SR-143]
+title = "Queue overlap is made visible, never blocked"
+sn_refs = ["SN-025"]
+requirement = """The validator shall report, warn-only and never in the exit code, pairs of OPEN work items that overlap by near-identical title, shared requirement citation, or a shared spec of record."""
+rationale = """A queue that silently grows two rows for one job is the failure; a checker that cannot tell a legitimate split from a duplicate must not block on the difference. Overlap is frequently correct — two rows may answer one requirement from different sides — so the whole contribution of the mechanical rung is visibility, and the judgement half belongs to an adjudicator with the scope in front of it."""
+acceptance_criteria = """Each overlapping OPEN pair is named once per signal with both ids, in deterministic order; a closed row never conflicts; the title bar is high enough that ordinary unrelated work is silent and a shared stopword is not a signal; the findings never join the exit code, not even under --strict."""
+priority = "S"
+verification = "Test"
+status = "Draft"
+phase = 5
+area = "Process"
+
+[requirement.SR-144]
+title = "Every lane close is a terminal state with an immutable record"
+sn_refs = ["SN-027"]
+requirement = """A lane that cannot finish shall close into a TERMINAL `partial/` state with one immutable per-close report naming the claimed outcome, the reason, the commit range and the keep/discard split, and shall merge like any other branch. A report declaring neither a split nor an explicit deferral shall be refused, and a second close of the same event shall be refused rather than overwriting the first."""
+rationale = """Five successive dedup mechanisms leaked because each reconstructed the return event from a MUTABLE proxy; a document that never moves dissolves the class instead of mitigating it. The refusals are what make "immutable" a property rather than a claim. The split is a field because its absence merged rejected code onto trunk on 2026-08-03: silence about it is different from saying "I could not judge", and only silence is unactionable."""
+acceptance_criteria = """A partial close is clean under the real validator (the terminal Deliverable rule is exempt, because an early close leaves the spec's definition byte-identical by design); a terminal spec with no report is LOUD rather than silently un-disposed; a second close is refused and leaves no residue in the lane; a multi-spec close that refuses restores every report it wrote."""
+priority = "S"
+verification = "Test"
+status = "Draft"
+phase = 5
+area = "Process"
+
+[requirement.SR-145]
+title = "A lane's outcome is a claim; the adjudicator overrules by minting"
+sn_refs = ["SN-027"]
+requirement = """Every `partial` and `cancelled` close shall earn one disposition row, keyed on the close's own immutable report path; a `complete` close shall be spot-checked at a declared rate rather than gated. Continuing partial work shall mean DRAFTING A SUCCESSOR carrying explicit lineage, never re-opening a terminal row."""
+rationale = """The lane is entitled to assert what happened; what it MEANS is the adjudicator's, and making that authoritative by minting keeps history honest — the original move is never reversed in place. Keying the disposition on the report PATH rather than on any field of it is what makes re-sweep idempotence correct: an earlier cut keyed on the report's mutable outcome field and re-minted on every edit. Adjudicating every green close would rebuild the verdict gate under a new name, over work two gates already judged."""
+acceptance_criteria = """A partial or cancelled close mints exactly one disposition; a re-sweep mints none; a genuinely second close is a second report and so a second row; a disposition row never mints a disposition for itself (refused at both ends); a successor's lineage survives the id change."""
+priority = "S"
+verification = "Test"
+status = "Draft"
+phase = 5
+area = "Process"
+
+[requirement.SR-146]
+title = "Prompts are reviewable files with a per-session audit trail"
+sn_refs = ["SN-005"]
+requirement = """Every prompt the loop launches shall be a shipped file with strictly filled slots, listed by digest in a freshness-gated generated catalogue, with each session recording which template it used and the fingerprint of what that rendered to."""
+rationale = """Prose steers the sessions this loop launches and had been reviewable only by reading Python source, which makes the process trusted rather than inspectable. The audit trail is the part that makes the move useful rather than cosmetic: without a per-session digest, "which instruction did this session see" stays an inference. (The argv-vs-shell-string transport decision is design, and lives one tier down in LLR-163.)"""
+acceptance_criteria = """An unknown or unfilled slot is a refusal; a missing shipped template is a named preflight refusal rather than a first-session crash; the catalogue lists every shipped prompt with its digest and `--check` fails when stale; a line-ending difference is not a digest change; an operator override is named in the session record rather than only in the launch flags."""
+priority = "S"
+verification = "Test"
+status = "Draft"
+phase = 5
+area = "Process"
+
+[requirement.SR-147]
+title = "One machine-parseable carrier for the requirement spine"
+sn_refs = ["SN-002", "SN-012"]
+requirement = """The kit shall carry the SN->SR->LLR->TC spine in ONE machine-parseable carrier rather than two (stakeholder needs as markdown prose tables, the other three tiers as CSV), and shall provide a mechanical converter that proves the conversion cell-for-cell over the live registries BEFORE the authority flip - refusing, with a nonzero exit that names the row and the field, any conversion in which a cell does not survive the round-trip."""
+rationale = """The two-carrier split has no recorded rationale and costs on both sides. Reading 32 need rows takes ~166 code lines across 14 functions in 8 modules - six bespoke scanners, two of them F5 twins pinned by nothing but a docstring, which have already drifted once and rendered a phantom root in the dashboard - while reading all 436 SR+LLR+TC rows takes csv.DictReader. CSV also cannot represent the cells that actually exist: five hold a literal pipe, 552 hold commas, the longest is 1,553 characters, and an embedded newline has no representation at all. A structured carrier turns three integrity rules into properties of the parse - a duplicate id becomes a decode error, a ref list becomes a typed array (retiring the split-on-whitespace rule that read `and` as an orphan), and an empty cell becomes an absent key, so `unset` and `set to empty` stop being the same value. The converter is the migration's evidence, not its convenience: SR-129's 140-cell lesson is that an unproven representation change is exactly where a registry silently loses cells."""
+acceptance_criteria = """migrate_carrier.py --check over the live registries exits 0 reporting the round-trip clean for every row of every tier; a cell that is dropped, truncated, re-typed or re-ordered is REFUSED with exit 1 and a finding naming the registry, the row id and the field; and the emitted text re-parses with tomllib."""
+priority = "C"
+verification = "Test"
+status = "Draft"
+phase = 5
+area = "Trajectory"
+
